1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,863
Add an AI Agent node after your form trigger, attach a chat model the same way you

2
00:00:04,863 --> 00:00:07,284
did in Section 3, and open the Prompt field.

3
00:00:07,284 --> 00:00:11,742
Set the User Message to pull in your three form fields: meeting title, attendees,

4
00:00:11,742 --> 00:00:13,172
and the transcript itself.

5
00:00:13,172 --> 00:00:17,795
One assembly detail matters more here than it did for email: label each piece as you

6
00:00:17,795 --> 00:00:22,363
drag it in, so the message reads Meeting title, colon, the title, Attendees, colon,

7
00:00:22,363 --> 00:00:25,004
the names, Transcript, colon, and then the text.

8
00:00:25,004 --> 00:00:25,225
Why?

9
00:00:25,225 --> 00:00:28,581
Because the model receives everything as one stream of words.

10
00:00:28,581 --> 00:00:33,204
Unlabelled, the attendee names just dissolve into the opening of the transcript, and

11
00:00:33,204 --> 00:00:37,607
the agent loses the one clue it needs to turn "Sam will send the figures" into a

12
00:00:37,607 --> 00:00:38,212
real owner.

13
00:00:38,212 --> 00:00:41,624
Three little labels, and each field arrives meaning something.

14
00:00:41,624 --> 00:00:45,421
It is the same point-not-paste habit from Section 3, plus a name tag.

15
00:00:45,971 --> 00:00:48,869
Notice the shape of this prompt against Section 3's.

16
00:00:48,869 --> 00:00:51,043
That one told the model what to ignore.

17
00:00:51,043 --> 00:00:53,941
This one tells it what it is not allowed to make up.

18
00:00:53,941 --> 00:00:58,343
Both are the same habit, aimed at a different failure mode: an inbox agent that

19
00:00:58,343 --> 00:01:02,913
over-flags is annoying, but a notes agent that invents a deadline nobody agreed to

20
00:01:02,913 --> 00:01:05,811
sends someone chasing a promise that was never made.

21
00:01:06,361 --> 00:01:11,092
Three phrases in there are carrying the load, and all three are transplants from the

22
00:01:11,092 --> 00:01:11,993
job description.

23
00:01:11,993 --> 00:01:16,667
"Explicitly agreed to do" is the line between commitments and intentions: calls are

24
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:21,115
full of "we should really look at that", and none of it is an action item until

25
00:01:21,115 --> 00:01:22,242
someone says I will.

26
00:01:22,242 --> 00:01:26,803
The agent needed that boundary stated, because politeness reads like agreement to

27
00:01:26,803 --> 00:01:29,112
anything that has never sat in a meeting.

28
00:01:29,112 --> 00:01:33,504
The other two are escape hatches, and they are the anti-invention ceiling made

29
00:01:33,504 --> 00:01:34,124
mechanical.

30
00:01:34,124 --> 00:01:38,854
A model fills vacuums with plausible content unless the honest empty answer has been

31
00:01:38,854 --> 00:01:43,584
given a legal shape, so you give it one at the line level and one at the list level.

32
00:01:43,584 --> 00:01:48,258
Now "no date" is a correct output, not a gap begging to be filled, and a meandering

33
00:01:48,258 --> 00:01:52,876
call with no commitments comes back as exactly that, instead of three manufactured

34
00:01:52,876 --> 00:01:54,284
items to justify the run.

35
00:01:54,284 --> 00:01:57,832
Write these hatches into every extraction prompt you ever make.

36
00:01:57,832 --> 00:02:02,449
They cost a sentence each and they retire the failure mode you are least likely to

37
00:02:02,449 --> 00:02:03,181
catch by eye.

38
00:02:03,731 --> 00:02:07,554
You are not being fussy by specifying the output format this precisely.

39
00:02:07,554 --> 00:02:11,861
You are removing one more thing you would otherwise have to check by hand, every

40
00:02:11,861 --> 00:02:13,368
single time this agent runs.

41
00:02:13,368 --> 00:02:15,468
Where does the strictness stop, though?

42
00:02:15,468 --> 00:02:16,760
At the words themselves.

43
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:21,013
The format pins the shape of each line, never the phrasing inside it: the agent

44
00:02:21,013 --> 00:02:25,535
still describes each action in whatever words the call used, which is what keeps the

45
00:02:25,535 --> 00:02:27,904
list readable as English rather than a code.

46
00:02:27,904 --> 00:02:29,842
Strict scaffolding, natural filling.

47
00:02:29,842 --> 00:02:33,503
That balance is worth copying into every format line you ever write.

48
00:02:33,503 --> 00:02:37,487
The owner-in-brackets convention has a quiet payoff too: a fixed format is

49
00:02:37,487 --> 00:02:38,026
scannable.

50
00:02:38,026 --> 00:02:42,494
Priya reads Monday's list by skimming the brackets, hers versus everyone else's, in

51
00:02:42,494 --> 00:02:43,517
about four seconds.

52
00:02:43,517 --> 00:02:47,878
And a fixed format is what makes the next growth step possible without a rebuild:

53
00:02:47,878 --> 00:02:52,185
the day you want these lines appended to a tracking sheet instead of read from a

54
00:02:52,185 --> 00:02:56,707
screen, a shape that never varies is the difference between one small node added and

55
00:02:56,707 --> 00:02:57,730
a parsing headache.

56
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:02,198
You will see exactly that pattern in the next section's build, where a sheet is the

57
00:03:02,198 --> 00:03:02,844
whole point.

58
00:03:02,844 --> 00:03:03,867
Formats are boring.

59
00:03:03,867 --> 00:03:06,290
Boring is what downstream steps can stand on.

60
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:11,124
It is worth one beat on why these two absences are load bearing, not laziness.

61
00:03:11,124 --> 00:03:15,738
Memory sounds like an upgrade until you picture its failure: an agent that remembers

62
00:03:15,738 --> 00:03:20,187
last week's call can blend it into this week's, and a commitment from a fortnight

63
00:03:20,187 --> 00:03:23,043
ago resurfaces on today's list wearing today's date.

64
00:03:23,043 --> 00:03:26,723
Each transcript judged alone makes that impossible by construction.

65
00:03:26,723 --> 00:03:31,118
A calendar tool sounds even more helpful, and it is also this build's first step

66
00:03:31,118 --> 00:03:35,512
onto the trust ladder's higher rungs: reaching into a second system is new blast

67
00:03:35,512 --> 00:03:38,478
radius, and it has not been through a job description.

68
00:03:38,478 --> 00:03:42,872
Section 6 adds a tool to an agent properly, on purpose, with the paperwork done.

69
00:03:42,872 --> 00:03:44,575
Here, restraint is the feature.

70
00:03:44,575 --> 00:03:48,859
With the prompt written, your agent can now read a transcript and extract what

71
00:03:48,859 --> 00:03:50,177
actually happened in it.

72
00:03:50,177 --> 00:03:54,736
One last read-through before you test: say the whole briefing aloud, Priya's habit,

73
00:03:54,736 --> 00:03:59,240
and listen for any sentence you would not say to a person taking over your meeting

74
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:00,174
notes for a week.

75
00:04:00,174 --> 00:04:04,623
If a line only makes sense to you because you wrote it, it will not make sense to

76
00:04:04,623 --> 00:04:05,557
the model either.

77
00:04:05,557 --> 00:04:09,676
Next lecture proves the build, without spending a real AI call to find out.
